This is exactly where **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)** comes into play. The key principle is:

> **Never send all document chunks to the AI. Retrieve only the chunks that are semantically relevant to the current story and prompt.**

For your StoryLens platform, I recommend a **multi-stage context retrieval pipeline** rather than a simple vector similarity search.

---

# Recommended Context Retrieval Pipeline

```text
User Reviews Story
        │
        ▼
Story Context Builder
        │
        ├── Story Text
        ├── Acceptance Criteria
        ├── Feature
        ├── Epic
        ├── User Prompt
        └── Repository Context
                │
                ▼
Semantic Query Generator
                │
                ▼
Retrieve Candidate Chunks
                │
                ▼
Metadata Filtering
                │
                ▼
Re-ranking
                │
                ▼
Top 10-20 Chunks
                │
                ▼
Prompt Builder
                │
                ▼
Bedrock Claude
```

---

# Step 1 – Build a Semantic Query

Don't search using just the story title.

Instead build a search query from multiple sources.

Example Story

```text
As a payment operator,
I want to reverse an ACH payment
so that failed settlements can be corrected.
```

Context

```
Feature
Payment Recall

Business Rules
Settlement cutoff

Acceptance Criteria

Repository

Database tables

APIs

MQ topics
```

The semantic query becomes

```text
ACH payment reversal

Payment recall

Settlement

Cutoff

Validation

Retry

Audit

Entitlement

ACH

Business rules
```

This retrieves much better matches.

---

# Step 2 – Metadata Filtering

Before vector search, reduce the search space.

Example

Instead of searching every document

Search only

```
Workspace

Payments

Feature

Payment Recall

Document Type

BRD

Excel

API Spec

Version

Latest Approved
```

Now your vector search is operating on a much smaller and more relevant corpus.

---

# Step 3 – Hybrid Search

Don't rely solely on embeddings.

Use:

```
BM25 Keyword Search

+

Vector Search

+

Metadata Filter

+

Recency

+

Document Priority
```

Hybrid search generally performs better for enterprise documentation because it combines exact keyword matches with semantic similarity.

---

# Step 4 – Re-rank the Results

Vector search might return:

```
Chunk A

0.92

Chunk B

0.91

Chunk C

0.90

Chunk D

0.89
```

But Chunk D might actually be the most useful because it explicitly mentions "ACH reversal."

Apply a reranker that considers:

* Story text
* Acceptance criteria
* Business rules
* User question

This produces a higher-quality final set.

---

