## For your banking platform, I'd go one step further: "Story Knowledge Graph"

Instead of retrieving chunks based only on similarity, build a graph of relationships.

```
Story
│
├── Feature
├── Epic
├── Business Rules
├── BRD Sections
├── Excel Rows
├── API Endpoints
├── Database Tables
├── Repository Classes
├── Previous Stories
├── Test Cases
├── Confluence Pages
└── Related Defects
```

Each node stores both metadata and embeddings. When a user opens **PAY-1023**, the system traverses the graph to gather the most relevant knowledge, then applies semantic ranking to fit within the token budget before sending the prompt to Bedrock.

This graph-based retrieval combines explicit project relationships (for example, "this story belongs to this feature") with semantic search, producing more accurate and explainable context than relying on vector similarity alone. Given your goal of building an enterprise platform for corporate banking, this architecture will scale much better as repositories, documents, and JIRA projects grow.
